<h2>San Antonio Factors That Affect Claim Value</h2>
<p>Local conditions matter. Many San Antonio neighborhoods were hit by more than one hailstorm over the years; NOAA logged 63 Bexar County hail reports in 2016 alone, and 45 in each of 2021 and 2023. A roof that has been patched before, or that uses a shingle no longer made, may not be repairable in a way that matches. Homes with stucco, limestone, or brick veneer can need specialized trades. Older pier-and-beam houses in historic districts may involve materials and methods a standard estimate does not include. And after a large storm, demand for roofers can push local prices up for months.</p>
<p>Repairs in the City of San Antonio generally require permits through the Development Services Department, and suburban cities like Schertz, Boerne, and Leon Valley have their own offices. Code-required items may be payable only to the extent your policy includes ordinance or law coverage. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page explains the basics.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Repair vs. Replace and Matching</h2>
<p>One of the largest sources of disagreement on an underpaid insurance claim in San Antonio is whether damaged materials should be repaired or replaced. An insurer may approve replacing a handful of shingles, a few siding panels, or one section of flooring. That can be reasonable when matching materials are available and the surrounding material is in good condition. It becomes harder when the product has been discontinued, when older shingles are too brittle to work with without breaking, or when new material would clearly not match the color or profile of what remains.</p>
<p>Policies handle matching differently, and the answer depends on the policy language and the facts. Useful evidence includes photos showing the condition of surrounding material, manufacturer or supplier information about product availability, and a contractor's written explanation of why a repair is or is not practical. Presenting this clearly gives the insurer a specific reason to reconsider rather than a general request for more money.</p>

<h2>Understanding the Payment Math</h2>
<p>Even an accurate estimate can produce a check that looks too small. Here is how many replacement cost claims are paid:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV):</strong> the estimated cost to repair or replace with like kind and quality.</li>
<li><strong>Minus depreciation:</strong> withheld for age and condition, giving actual cash value (ACV).</li>
<li><strong>Minus deductible:</strong> often a percentage wind/hail deductible on Texas policies.</li>
<li><strong>Minus prior payments:</strong> anything already paid on the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Recoverable depreciation:</strong> may be released after repairs are completed, within any deadline the policy sets.</li>
</ol>
<p>Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> lets you plug in your own numbers, and our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> covers the details.</p>

<h2>How a San Antonio Public Adjuster Can Help With an Underpaid Claim</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Estimate review.</strong> We compare the insurer's estimate against the damage, line by line. See <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Re-inspection.</strong> We inspect and document missed damage with photos, measurements, and, where useful, Matterport 3D imaging.</li>
<li><strong>Our own estimate.</strong> We prepare an Xactimate estimate in the same format the insurer uses, so differences are clear.</li>
<li><strong>Supplement or reopen request.</strong> We submit the documentation and follow up. See <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Next steps if negotiation stalls.</strong> If the only dispute is the amount of loss, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be an option to discuss.</li>
</ol>
<p>We cannot guarantee that the insurer will pay more. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>What You Can Do Now</h2>
<ul>
<li>Get the insurer's full estimate, including the summary page and any photo report.</li>
